Optimized Locking in SQL Server 2025: Improving Concurrency with TID Locking and LAQ
Introduction

Locking is fundamental to SQL Server's transaction processing model. When multiple sessions access and modify the same data concurrently, SQL Server uses locks to maintain transaction isolation and protect data consistency.

Locks themselves are not the problem. The challenge appears in highly concurrent systems when transactions acquire large numbers of row or page locks and retain them until the transaction completes.

Consider a transaction updating thousands of rows. Traditionally, SQL Server might retain many exclusive locks until COMMIT or ROLLBACK. Under sufficient workload, this can increase lock memory consumption, contribute to lock escalation, and cause otherwise independent transactions to wait.

SQL Server 2025 introduces Optimized Locking, a significant change to how locks associated with data modifications can be managed.

Optimized locking is designed to:

Reduce the number of locks retained for the duration of a transaction.
Reduce lock memory consumption.
Reduce lock escalation.
Reduce unnecessary blocking between concurrent writers.
Improve concurrency for high-volume transactional workloads.

The feature does not eliminate locking. Instead, it changes when certain locks are acquired, how long they are retained, and how SQL Server protects uncommitted modifications.

Optimized locking primarily relies on two mechanisms:

Transaction ID (TID) Locking
Lock After Qualification (LAQ)

Understanding the distinction between these two mechanisms is essential when evaluating the feature.

Traditional SQL Server Locking

Suppose we have an order-processing table:

CREATE TABLE dbo.OrderQueue
(
    OrderID      int            NOT NULL,
    CustomerID   int            NOT NULL,
    OrderStatus  varchar(20)    NOT NULL,
    Amount       decimal(12,2)  NOT NULL,
    ModifiedDate datetime2      NOT NULL,

    CONSTRAINT PK_OrderQueue
        PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (OrderID)
);
GO

Now imagine a transaction modifies a large number of orders:

BEGIN TRANSACTION;

UPDATE dbo.OrderQueue
SET OrderStatus = 'Processing',
    ModifiedDate = SYSUTCDATETIME()
WHERE CustomerID = 1001;

-- Transaction intentionally left open

Without optimized locking, SQL Server can retain row or key locks for modified records until the transaction ends.

Conceptually:

Transaction
    |
    +-- X lock -> Row 1
    +-- X lock -> Row 2
    +-- X lock -> Row 3
    +-- X lock -> Row 4
    +-- X lock -> Row 5
    |
    ...
    |
    +-- X lock -> Row N
    |
   COMMIT

Those locks protect the uncommitted changes, but maintaining many locks has a cost.

SQL Server may also decide that maintaining thousands of fine-grained locks is too expensive and attempt lock escalation.

Optimized locking changes this behavior.

Transaction ID Locking

The first major component is Transaction ID locking, commonly called TID locking.

Every transaction has a transaction identifier. Under optimized locking, modified rows can be associated with the transaction that last modified them.

Instead of retaining an exclusive row or key lock for every modified row until the transaction completes, SQL Server can release many row and page locks shortly after the physical modification and retain an exclusive lock associated with the transaction itself.

Conceptually, traditional locking may look like this:

Transaction 75

X KEY -> Order 1001
X KEY -> Order 1002
X KEY -> Order 1003
X KEY -> Order 1004
X KEY -> Order 1005
...
X KEY -> Order 5000

With optimized locking, the long-duration protection becomes closer to:

Transaction 75

             XACT
               |
          X TID Lock
               |
       Transaction 75

The individual modification locks can be short-lived while the transaction-level TID lock protects the uncommitted work.

This is an important distinction:

SQL Server is not removing transaction protection. It is changing the resource used to maintain that protection.

If another transaction attempts to modify data belonging to an active transaction, SQL Server can determine the transaction responsible for that row and wait on the corresponding transaction resource.

Lock After Qualification

TID locking primarily reduces the locks retained after rows are modified.

Lock After Qualification (LAQ) addresses another source of contention: locks acquired while SQL Server searches for rows to modify.

Consider:

UPDATE dbo.OrderQueue
SET OrderStatus = 'Completed'
WHERE OrderID = 200;

SQL Server first needs to determine which rows satisfy:

WHERE OrderID = 200

Traditionally, during certain execution plans, SQL Server can acquire update locks while examining candidate rows.

That means a transaction might encounter a locked row that it does not ultimately need.

LAQ changes this sequence.

Instead of:

Find row
   |
Acquire U lock
   |
Evaluate predicate
   |
Does row qualify?
   |
  Yes
   |
Update row

SQL Server can perform:

Read latest committed version
          |
Evaluate predicate
          |
Does row qualify?
       /      \
     No        Yes
     |          |
   Skip       Acquire lock
                |
              Update

SQL Server therefore attempts to qualify first and lock afterward.

This can substantially reduce blocking when concurrent transactions are updating different rows.

LAQ requires Read Committed Snapshot Isolation (RCSI).
